{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And he shall burn the cow before his eyes, et its skin.</b> It interrupted with the phrase ״לְעֵינָיו״ (before his eyes) between the mention of the cow and the mention of its parts — which are its skin, etc. This may be explained according to what they said in tractate Parah (chapter 4), and which Rambam wrote in chapter 4 of the Laws of the Red Heifer: that the mitzvah is to burn it whole, and if he skinned it and cut it up and burned all of it, it is valid — end quote. Therefore the verse separated the preferred mode of performing the act and said <b>and he shall burn the cow</b> — meaning, all of it together; and even if he transgressed and skinned it, etc., and made its skin separately and its flesh separately and its dung separately, he has likewise not invalidated it, and that is the phrase ״אֶת עוֹרָהּ וְאֶת״ etc." "<b>Its dung he shall burn.</b> It went back to say <b>he shall burn</b> a second time, and was not satisfied with what it said at first, ״וְשָׂרַף,״ according to what I explained — that the verse speaks of two modes: one, burning the cow whole; and the second, after it has been skinned and cut up. Therefore it says <b>יִשְׂרֹף</b> corresponding to the second mode. It may further be explained according to what they said in the Tosefta of Parah (chapter 2), and which Rambam wrote in chapter 4 of the Laws of the Red Heifer, and this is his language: if a piece of its skin detached, etc., even a hair's worth of its hair like an olive-bulk, he must put it back, and if he did not put it back, it is disqualified — end quote. And that is the phrase <b>יִשְׂרֹף</b> a second time, to say that even if it detached he must put it back and burn it, as a requirement."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:04.797461301Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}